  "query_preview": "..\nThe latest round of thunderstorms has begun affecting the area early\nthis afternoon. Based on latest trends, areas near and south of I-70\nlook to see the greatest risk for severe weather with this latest\nround. The environment continues to be supportive of \ndamaging/destructive wind gusts, where localized gusts could reach \n70 to 80 mph leading to more significant local damage compared to 60 \nmph wind gusts. In addition, there is the risk for isolated tornadoes\nwithin line segments. \n\nStorm motion will tend to be to the southeast, limiting training, however\ndue to high rainfall rates localized flash flooding will be possible\nparticularly in any more vulnerable areas that have received a lot \nof rainfall within the past 24 hours, such as portions of \ncentral/east central Ohio. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nInitial convection should move through the area by early this \nevening, however any reprieve may be brief as plenty of high \nresolution guidance suggests additional storms moving into the area \nor developing over the area. The most likely area for this to occur \nappears to be over southeast Indiana/southwest Ohio/northern Kentucky\nhowever confidence in exact placement of new nocturnal convection is\nrather low and it is possible this could be placed further north. \n\nWherever the new convection orients itself will have a low end severe\nrisk for damaging winds. A more significant flash flood risk may \nemerge especially if activity aligns northwest to southeast and \nrepetitively moves across the same area. At this time, a Flood Watch \nremains across the entire area through tonight/early Wednesday \nmorning. \n\nIt's unclear whether the balance of Wednesday may see a \nanother lull in convection, or if another round may impact at least \npart of the area. If another round does occur, it could be late in \nthe day or Wednesday night, with continued risks for flash flooding \nand at least a low end severe risk for damaging wind. Later updates \nwill have to refine and resolve timing/placement details of any late\nWednesday/Wednesday night convection.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3) \nThe active pattern may continue through the end of the week, but\ndetails on individual disturbances and timing of hazardous weather \nimpacts are uncertain. Either way, we continue with a chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms through Friday. Guidance does suggest the \npotential for drying to occur this weekend, and while we can't \ncomplelty rule out a thunderstorm, the trend should be down for the \nweekend.",
